== Bounded Clock Sweep and the Cooling-Stage Evictor ==

=== Request for Comments [RFC] PROPOSED / EXPERIMENTAL &mdash; NOT MERGED ===

This page describes a four-patch series against the PostgreSQL buffer manager.
It is '''experimental''': it has not been reviewed on pgsql-hackers in this
form, it changes the hottest path in the buffer manager, and the production
evidence for the problem it solves is still partly second-hand.  Do not run it
on anything you care about.

The series addresses a failure mode in the existing clock sweep that is not a
throughput problem and does not show up in any standard benchmark: under the
right conditions a '''single buffer read can sweep the entire buffer pool''',
and the cost grows linearly with <code>shared_buffers</code>.

'''The fix does not require changing the replacement state.'''  The bound has
been measured applied to the unmodified 0..5 <code>usage_count</code> sweep --
two files, no new buffer state, no change to the background writer or the ring
buffers -- and it produces the same O(1) worst case as the 1-bit variant.  The
bound and the 1-bit cooling state are orthogonal and separately adoptable; see
[[#Measured results|the five-arm table]].

---

== The problem ==

A buffer becomes eligible for eviction only when its <code>usage_count</code>
reaches zero.  Two facts combine badly:

* <code>PinBuffer()</code> saturates <code>usage_count</code> back to
  <code>BM_MAX_USAGE_COUNT</code> (5) on every access.
* The clock hand decrements by one per visit, and a decrement counts as
  progress, so it resets <code>trycounter</code>.

Let ''T'' be the time for the hand to complete one pass of the pool.  A page
must then go roughly ''5T'' without a single access before it can be reclaimed.
''T'' grows with <code>NBuffers</code>.  On a large pool, any workload that
touches much of the pool more often than once per five passes re-promotes
buffers faster than the hand demotes them.  The population of evictable buffers
collapses, and because cooling resets the try counter there is '''no bound''' on
how long one <code>StrategyGetBuffer()</code> call will search.

Modelling the accesses to a page as a Poisson process of rate ''r'', the
fraction of the pool that is evictable is approximately
<code>e^(-k·r·T)</code>, with ''k''=5 for the current counter.  The eviction
candidate density therefore decays '''exponentially''' in both the access rate
and the pool size.  At ''rT''=1 that is 0.0067 of the pool; at ''rT''=2 it is
3.4e-5.

This is the mechanism behind reports of large instances where a scan stalls for
seconds with no I/O wait and no lock contention.

== What does ''not'' fix it ==

Replacing the 0..5 counter with a single HOT/COOL bit (''k''=1 instead of
''k''=5) moves the cliff out by about 5x in ''r·T'' and reduces measured sweep
amplification by ~1.75x.  '''It does not remove the cliff.'''  Measured worst
case is still 0.998 of <code>NBuffers</code>.  Fewer states is a better
constant, not a bound.  This correction matters: the 1-bit evictor was
originally proposed on simplicity grounds and benchmarked as a throughput wash,
and neither argument is the real one.

== The series ==

=== 0001 &mdash; cooling-stage evictor ===

Replace <code>usage_count</code> with a one-bit cooling state, reusing the same
field so the 64-bit buffer-state layout is unchanged.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! transition !! stock !! this patch
|-
| admit || <code>usage_count = 1</code> || '''COOL''' (probation)
|-
| pin (no strategy) || <code>min(uc+1, 5)</code> || COOL &rarr; HOT
|-
| pin (ring) || <code>max(uc, 1)</code> || no change
|-
| sweep visit, HOT || <code>uc--</code>, keep scanning || HOT &rarr; COOL, keep scanning
|-
| sweep visit, COOL || if <code>uc==0</code>, CAS-claim || CAS-claim
|}

Demotion and reclamation happen on '''separate ticks''', so a buffer always
survives the pass that cools it and gets one full sweep of grace in which a new
access can rescue it.  A ring access deliberately does not promote, which
preserves the existing rule that ring buffers must not evict others from the
pool and keeps <code>GetBufferFromRing()</code>'s reuse test correct.

=== 0002 &mdash; the bound (the patch that matters) ===

Once a single <code>StrategyGetBuffer()</code> call has demoted
<code>BUF_COOL_CLAIM_THRESHOLD</code> buffers without finding one evictable,
the pool is ''demonstrably'' hotter than the hand can grind down.  From that
point in the same call, the sweep claims the next unpinned buffer outright
instead of merely demoting it.

The cost is evicting a buffer that had not finished probation, which is the
right trade only when the alternative is an unbounded scan; the threshold is
derived from the cache line size, not exposed as a tunable, and is set well
above what a healthy workload reaches.  Under no pressure the counter never
reaches it and behaviour is '''unchanged'''.  A shared counter
(<code>numCoolClaims</code>) records how often this happens, which is the
signal that the pool is undersized for its access rate.

=== 0003 &mdash; background staging ===

The background writer's cleaning scan already walks buffers ahead of the hand
and already holds each buffer header lock, so demoting an unpinned HOT buffer
as it passes costs one masked store on work being done anyway, with no CAS.

Crucially it stages candidates '''only while <code>numCoolClaims</code> is
rising'''.  Cooling buffers nobody is waiting for would shorten every buffer's
probation for no benefit &mdash; the mistake a fixed background cooler makes.
Staged buffers count toward <code>reusable_buffers</code>, so the existing
demand estimate bounds the work and it cannot run away and cool the pool.

=== 0004 &mdash; why the rings stay ===

COOL admission makes a read-once page self-evicting, which is the
pollution-avoidance job the <code>BufferAccessStrategy</code> rings were
introduced for.  It does not replace their other roles:
<code>BAS_BULKREAD</code> drives <code>StrategyRejectBuffer()</code>, and
<code>BAS_BULKWRITE</code>/<code>BAS_VACUUM</code> dirty their buffers on first
touch, so admission policy cannot make them self-evicting.  All strategies are
retained; see [[#Vacuum ring|the vacuum measurement]] below for what is and is
not known.

---

== Measured results ==

Two bare-metal instances: '''z1d.metal''' (48 cores, 2 NUMA nodes, datadir on
local NVMe RAID0) and '''r8i.metal-96xl''' (384 cores, 6 NUMA nodes).
<code>debugoptimized</code>, <code>cassert=off</code>, huge pages on.

'''Workload''' (this is the part that matters &mdash; standard benchmarks do not
reproduce the problem): point lookups with
<code>random_zipfian(s=1.10)</code> over a table 1.15x
<code>shared_buffers</code>, 3 runs per point, counters sampled after a 60s
warmup.  Uniform access over a working set much larger than the pool '''does
not''' reproduce it, because each page's individual access rate is then too low
for anything to become un-evictable.

=== The three axes, five arms ===

<code>max ticks</code> = the most clock-hand advances any single allocation
performed.  <code>ampl.</code> = total advances / buffers reclaimed.  All five
arms differ '''only''' in the eviction path and carry the identical instrument;
3 runs per point, counters sampled after a 60s warmup.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! s_b !! arm !! ampl. !! max ticks !! max / NBuffers !! claims !! hit %
|-
| 8GB || master 0..5 || 5.87 || 1,048,614 || '''1.0000''' || 0 || 99.706
|-
| 8GB || '''0..5 + claim''' || 5.25 || '''34''' || 0.00003 || 6,226 || 99.691
|-
| 8GB || 1-bit || 3.34 || 1,046,554 || '''0.9981''' || 0 || 99.707
|-
| 8GB || 1-bit + claim || 3.39 || '''35''' || 0.00003 || 1,485 || 99.707
|-
| 8GB || 1-bit + claim + bgw || 3.11 || '''34''' || 0.00003 || 1,919 || 99.684
|-
| 32GB || master 0..5 || 2.11 || 4,194,342 || '''1.0000''' || 0 || 99.564
|-
| 32GB || '''0..5 + claim''' || 4.26 || '''34''' || 0.00001 || 15,797 || 99.591
|-
| 32GB || 1-bit || 1.32 || 4,192,282 || '''0.9995''' || 0 || 99.618
|-
| 32GB || 1-bit + claim || 1.32 || '''34''' || 0.00001 || 0 || 99.617
|-
| 32GB || 1-bit + claim + bgw || 1.32 || '''33''' || 0.00001 || 0 || 99.617
|-
| 96GB || master 0..5 || 1.17 || 12,582,950 || '''1.0000''' || 0 || 99.410
|}

Two things to read off this table:

* '''Master's worst single allocation equals <code>NBuffers</code> to five
  significant figures at every pool size from 4GB to 96GB.'''  It is exactly one
  full pass of the pool, scaling linearly with <code>shared_buffers</code>:
  O(''NBuffers'').
* '''0002 makes it constant''' at 33-37 ticks regardless of pool size: O(1).
  That is a ~31,000x reduction at 32GB and ~370,000x at 96GB.

The result is '''NUMA-independent'''.  Master pins to exactly the same max-ticks
value on 2 nodes and on 6; node count changes throughput (613k vs 970k TPS) but
not the amplification or the tail.

=== What this is not ===

Throughput and hit ratio are '''flat across all four arms''' at every point
measured, within ~2% and with overlapping run-to-run ranges.  This is a
bounded-work and tail-latency change.  Installations with small pools, uniform
access patterns, or working sets much larger than
<code>shared_buffers</code> will see nothing.

=== The threshold is a measured dial, not a guess ===

&Theta; (<code>BUF_DECREMENT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD</code>) trades the tail against
replacement quality.  Rather than defend a constant, the whole curve was swept
at runtime over three decades (&Theta;=0 means never claim, i.e. stock):

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! &Theta; !! max ticks !! ampl. !! claims !! hot-set hit % !! hot pages evicted
|-
| 1 || 3 || 2.00 || 825,450 || &mdash; || &mdash;
|-
| 4 || 6 || 4.06 || 400,090 || 98.2313 || 4,519
|-
| 16 || 18 || 5.92 || 59,652 || &mdash; || &mdash;
|-
| 32 || 36 || 5.37 || 5,576 || 99.9671 || 11
|-
| '''128''' || '''130''' || 5.19 || 64 || '''99.9998''' || '''0'''
|-
| 1024 || 1,026 || 5.14 || 0 || &mdash; || &mdash;
|-
| 0 (none) || 61,532 || 5.11 || 0 || 100.0000 || 0
|}

'''<code>max ticks</code> &asymp; &Theta; + 2''' across three decades, so
&Theta; is a direct and predictable dial rather than an opaque heuristic.

The hot-set columns come from a workload built to make probation matter: a hot
set of 20% of the pool receiving 80% of queries, with a cold stream at 130% of
the pool forcing continuous eviction.  '''This closes the question of whether
premature eviction actually harms hit ratio: it does.'''  At &Theta;=4 the
claim path evicts 4,519 genuinely hot pages for 1.77 points of hot-set hit
ratio; at &Theta;=32 it evicts eleven; at &Theta;=128 it evicts none and the
cost is not measurable, while the tail is still bounded at 130 advances against
an unbounded scan's 10<sup>6</sup>.

'''&Theta;=128 is therefore the default.'''  Note that pool-wide hit ratio
would have hidden this effect -- it moves 0.65 points where the hot set moves
1.77 -- which is why replacement quality must be measured on the set the
workload actually re-reads.

=== Adapting the threshold is worse (negative result) ===

A fixed &Theta; picks one point on that curve, so adapting it is the obvious
next idea.  It was implemented and measured: per-backend AIMD starting at
&Theta;<sub>max</sub>=1024, halving on each forced claim, recovering by one on
each claim-free allocation.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! variant !! max ticks !! claims !! hit % !! ampl.
|-
| unbounded || 57,908 || 0 || 99.7083 || 5.02
|-
| '''fixed &Theta;=32''' || '''34''' || 7,702 || '''99.7023''' || 5.41
|-
| adaptive (AIMD) || 1,025 || 188,962 || 99.6675 || 5.60
|}

'''The controller is worse than a fixed value on both axes.'''  At ~600k
allocations/sec, recovery at +1 per claim-free allocation returns &Theta; to
its maximum in milliseconds, and claim-free allocations vastly outnumber
claims, so the loop sits near &Theta;<sub>max</sub> almost always (paying a
full 1024-advance scan on each starvation event) while re-entering back-off
often enough to claim 25x more than the fixed threshold.  It oscillates instead
of converging.

The underlying asymmetry: hit-ratio loss is paid on '''every''' claim,
continuously, while the unbounded scan is paid only by the rare allocation that
starves.  A controller whose recovery is fast relative to the arrival of
starvation events cannot exploit that, and since hit ratio already saturates at
&Theta;=128 there is nothing above it to win.  The experiment is preserved on
branch <code>adaptive-experiment</code>; the shippable code uses a fixed
&Theta;.

=== Vacuum ring ===

<code>vacuum_buffer_usage_limit = 0</code> already makes
<code>GetAccessStrategyWithSize()</code> return NULL, so whether vacuum's ring
still earns its keep is measurable '''without a patch'''.  With this series
applied (6 NUMA, s_b=8GB, scale 1000, 64 OLTP clients concurrent with one large
VACUUM, 3 runs alternated):

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! metric !! ring on !! ring off !! note
|-
| OLTP tps || 56,444 || 57,944 || ranges overlap &mdash; neutral
|-
| OLTP p99 (ms) || 4.29 || 4.38 || neutral
|-
| VACUUM wall clock (s) || 66.6 || '''19.9''' || ranges separate &mdash; 3.3x faster
|-
| buffer hit % || 96.02 || 96.10 || identical
|}

This is evidence '''for''' removability, and it contradicts the writeback-pacing
argument originally given for keeping the ring.  It is not enough to act on: one
workload shape, one pool size, one storage class, explicit VACUUM rather than
many concurrent autovacuum workers, and local NVMe rather than slower storage
that might reward pacing.  Removing <code>BAS_VACUUM</code> would also remove
<code>IOCONTEXT_VACUUM</code> and the <code>vacuum</code> context from
<code>pg_stat_io</code>, and render the documented
<code>vacuum_buffer_usage_limit</code> GUC meaningless.  The rings therefore
stay and the series changes replacement policy only.

---

== Reproducing it ==

No patch is required to observe the problem.  Size a pool large enough that a
pass takes real time, a working set slightly larger than the pool, and use
'''skewed''' access:

<pre>
shared_buffers = 8GB
huge_pages = on
autovacuum = off

-- table ~1.15x shared_buffers
CREATE TABLE w (id bigint, pad text) WITH (fillfactor=100);
INSERT INTO w SELECT g, repeat('x',100) FROM generate_series(1,82000000) g;
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX w_pk ON w (id);
VACUUM ANALYZE w;
</pre>

<pre>
# probe.sql
\set k random_zipfian(1, 82000000, 1.10)
SELECT pad FROM w WHERE id = :k;
</pre>

<pre>
pgbench -n -M prepared -f probe.sql -c 24 -j 24 -T 90
</pre>

Then ask the pool what state it is in:

<pre>
SELECT usage_count, buffers FROM pg_buffercache_usage_counts()
 ORDER BY usage_count;
</pre>

'''If the pool is piled at <code>usage_count</code> 5 with little or nothing at
0, the sweep has no candidates and every allocation is paying for the search.'''
That query is the cheapest diagnostic for whether this affects a given
installation, and it needs no patch and no restart.

== Status and open questions ==

* '''Experimental.''' Not reviewed on-list in this form.
* Production evidence that this costs real users is still second-hand; the
  <code>pg_buffercache_usage_counts()</code> histogram above is the ask.
* 0002 is independently useful '''on top of the existing 0..5 counter''' if the
  1-bit evictor is not wanted; the bound does not depend on the state change.
* The threshold is cache-line-derived and unmeasured for sensitivity.  Whether
  it wants to be adaptive is open.
* At high enough ''r·T'' even 0002 evicts buffers that have not served their
  probation.  Whether that measurably hurts hit ratio in a regime we have not
  constructed is unknown.
* Branches on <code>github.com/gburd/postgres</code>:
** <code>stock-claim</code> &mdash; '''the bound alone, against pristine
   master''' (two files; this is the one to review first)
** <code>bcs</code> &mdash; 1-bit evictor + bound + background staging
** <code>adaptive-experiment</code> &mdash; the AIMD negative result

